{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-130270",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,0,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xyz),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xyz),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xyz)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x-yz,\\,2y-xz,\\,2z-xy⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,0,-2) & =⟨2(1)-0(-2),2(0)-1(-2),2(-2)-1(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨2,2,-4⟩.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
